{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018QRRbFGcnw9itAoHnPmghZ","type":"tool_result","content":"/ux/set/bin/metais\nmetais 0.0.0-devel\nspecification 1.0\nDORA major ICT-related incident report — dora-incident-report 1.0.0\nRenders a financial entity's report on a major ICT-related incident under Regulation (EU) 2022/2554 (DORA), at each of its three stages, from the fields of the template of Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/302.\nMETAIS 1.0, packed, sha256:f28b6786597f244f2481f5749849b65767ff1d272ba691b1eea10384f8894754\n\nGuide\nA financial entity that classifies an ICT-related incident as major\nreports it to its competent authority three times, on one template: an\ninitial notification, an intermediate report and a final report.\nImplementing Regulation (EU) 2025/302 lays down the template in its\nAnnex I and its data glossary in its Annex II, which numbers every field,\ngives its type, and says at which stage it becomes mandatory. This\npackage carries that glossary as its schemas and renders each report as\nincident-report.md, every field under its number.\n\nSupply three inputs in a data directory:\n\n- stage, field 1.1, the type of submission: \"initial notification\",\n  \"intermediate report\" or \"final report\";\n- entity, section 1 of the template: who submits the report, for which\n  financial entity, and whom to contact;\n- incident, sections 2, 3 and 4: the incident itself.\n\nRead [what the stage decides](inputs/incident.md) before filling the\nincident in. The stage decides which fields are mandatory. Section 2's\nare mandatory at every stage, section 3's from the intermediate report\non, and section 4's in the final report. A field the stage does not yet\nrequire may be given early, and is then checked like any other.\n\nEach report updates the one before it. Keep one data directory per\nincident, and for each new report change stage, add the fields the stage\nadds and correct what has changed:\n\n    metais validate dora-incident-report --data incident\n    metais render dora-incident-report --data incident --out report\n\nvalidate reports every mandatory field the stage still lacks, and every\nvalue that does not fit its field's type, all at once. Each schema's\nproperties carry the glossary's field number as x-field and its type as\nx-field-type; metais schema dora-incident-report incident shows them.\n\nThe package renders the content of a report. It does not file it: send\nit to your competent authority in the form and through the channel that\nauthority prescribes. The package's [references](references/sources.md)\nname the legal texts it is written from and their reuse terms.\n\nInputs\n  stage — 1.1 Type of submission (required; stage.json, stage.yaml, stage.yml)\n    Which of the three reports on the incident this is: the initial notification, the intermediate report or the final report.\n  entity — The financial entity (required; entity.json, entity.yaml, entity.yml)\n    Section 1 of the template: the entity submitting the report, the financial entity affected, and whom to contact.\n  incident — The incident (required; incident.json, incident.yaml, incident.yml)\n    Sections 2, 3 and 4 of the template: the incident, as this stage's report states it.\n\nOutputs\n  incident-report.md ← template incident-report.md.liquid\n\nReferences\n  1 entry, 4.8 KiB\n  references/sources.md (4.8 KiB) — Sources and their terms\n\nExamples\n  An example is the author's illustration of the inputs, and of the outputs a render of them should produce, kept in the package to test it: not your data.\n  final: entity, incident, stage (with expected outputs)\n  initial: entity, incident, stage (with expected outputs)\n  intermediate: entity, incident, stage (with expected outputs)\n\nNext:\n  metais read <package> inputs/incident.md  — Reads what the input incident means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais schema <package> stage --fields  — Lists the fields of the input stage, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> entity --fields  — Lists the fields of the input entity, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> incident --fields  — Lists the fields of the input incident, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais grep <package> <pattern> references/  — Searches the package's references.\n  metais validate <package> --data <dir>  — Validates the inputs you gathered in a data directory.\n  metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>  — Renders the outputs from the validated inputs into a new directory.\n  metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>  — Renders an example from generated data, to preview the outputs; generated data is never a real document's facts.\n  metais sample <package> --out <dir>  — Writes generated data as a data directory, data to start from; generated data is never a real document's facts.\n  metais ls <package> examples/final/ -r  — Lists the first example's inputs and expected outputs. An example is the author's illustration of the inputs, and of the outputs a render of them should produce, kept in the package to test it: not your data.\n  metais unpack <package> --out <dir>  — Writes the package's source directory, to edit it and pack it again.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"531d25c6-1996-4e4a-ab1c-0f538ef59990","uuid":"e1a0fee8-6ac4-4ada-9038-a997e517b801","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:53:00.972Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/ux/set/bin/metais\nmetais 0.0.0-devel\nspecification 1.0\nDORA major ICT-related incident report — dora-incident-report 1.0.0\nRenders a