\tany\toptional\tenum=[\"malicious actions: deliberate internal actions\",\"malicious actions: deliberate physical damage/manipulation/theft\",\"malicious actions: fraudulent actions\",\"process failure: insufficient monitoring or failure of monitoring and control\",\"process failure: insufficient/unclear roles and responsibilities\",\"process failure: ICT risk management process failure\",\"process failure: insufficient or failure of ICT operations and ICT security operations\",\"process failure: insufficient or failure of ICT project management\",\"process failure: inadequacy of internal policies, procedures and documentation\",\"Process failure: inadequate ICT systems acquisition, development, and maintenance\",\"process failure: other (please specify)\",\"system failure: hardware capacity and performance\",\"system failure: hardware maintenance\",\"system failure: hardware obsolescence/ageing\",\"system failure: software compatibility/configuration\",\"system failure: software performance\",\"system failure: network configuration\",\"system failure: physical da[…+369 bytes]\t-\n/incident/root_cause_information\tstring\toptional\tminLength=1\t4.5 Information about the root causes of the incident\n/incident/service_downtime\tstring\toptional\tpattern=\"^[0-9]{2,3}:([01][0-9]|2[0-3]):[0-5][0-9]$\"\t3.16 Service downtime\n/incident/temporary_actions\tboolean\toptional\t-\t3.33 Temporary actions/measures taken or planned to be taken to recover from the incident\n/incident/temporary_actions_description\tstring\toptional\tminLength=1\t3.34 Description of any temporary actions and measures taken or planned to be taken to recover from the incident\n/incident/threats_and_techniques\tarray\toptional\tminItems=1 uniqueItems=true\t3.25 Threats and techniques used by the threat actor\n/incident/threats_and_techniques/*\tany\toptional\tenum=[\"Social engineering (including phishing)\",\"(D)DoS\",\"Identity theft\",\"Data encryption for impact, including ransomware\",\"Resource hijacking\",\"Data exfiltration and manipulation, including identity theft\",\"Data destruction\",\"Defacement\",\"Supply-chain attack\",\"Other (please specify)\"]\t-\n/incident/transactions_affected\tinteger\toptional\tminimum=0\t3.9 Number of affected transactions\n/incident/transactions_affected_percentage\tnumber\toptional\tmaximum=100 minimum=0\t3.10 Percentage of affected transactions\n/incident/transactions_affected_value\tnumber\toptional\tminimum=0\t3.11 Value of affected transactions\n/incident/geographical_spread_countries\tany\trequired\t-\t-\twhen /incident matches {\"properties\":{\"classification_criteria\":{\"contains\":{\"const\":\"geographical spread\"}}},\"required\":[\"classification_criteria\"]}\n\nNext:\n  metais schema <package> incident  — Prints the whole schema, with each field's description.\n  metais validate <package> --data <dir>  — Validates the inputs you gathered in a data directory against this schema.\n=== incident.md\n# The incident, stage by stage\n\nOne incident is reported three times, and each report states everything\nknown at its stage. The stage, field 1.1, decides which fields the report\nmust hold:\n\n| Stage | Mandatory |\n|---|---|\n| `initial notification` | the mandatory fields of section 1 (the entity) and section 2 |\n| `intermediate report` | those, and the mandatory fields of section 3 |\n| `final report` | those, and the mandatory fields of section 4 |\n\nEvery property of the entity and the incident is titled with its field's\nnumber and its name in the glossary, and carries the number as `x-field`\nand the glossary's type as `x-field-type`. Its description says what it\nholds and when it is mandatory.\n\n## Fields mandatory on a condition\n\nSome fields are mandatory only when another field shows they apply. The\npackage checks each condition the data itself shows:\n\n| When | Then also give |\n|---|---|\n| 1.5, the affected entity's name, is given | 1.6, its LEI |\n| 1.13, the parent undertaking's name, is given | 1.14, its LEI |\n| 2.5 includes geographical spread | 2.6; and from the intermediate report on, 3.18 and 3.19 |\n\nFrom the intermediate report on:\n\n| When | Then also give |\n|---|---|\n| 2.5 includes reputational impact | 3.13 and 3.14 |\n| 2.5 includes duration and service downtime | 3.17 |\n| 2.5 includes data losses | 3.20 and 3.21 |\n| 3.16, the service downtime, is given | 3.3 |\n| 3.23 includes Other (please specify) | 3.24 |\n| 3.23 includes Cybersecurity-related | 3.25 and 3.35 |\n| 3.25 includes Other (please specify) | 3.26 |\n| 3.28 is Yes | 3.29 |\n| 3.31 includes Other (please specify) | 3.32 |\n| 3.33 is true | 3.34 |\n\nIn the final report:\n\n| When | Then also give |\n|---|---|\n| 4.2 includes a category 4.3 refines: monitoring and control, ICT risk management, ICT operations and ICT security operations, or ICT systems acquisition, development and maintenance | 4.3 |\n| 4.2 includes an other (please specify) | 4.4 |\n| 4.15 is given | 4.16 |\n\nThe other conditional fields turn on facts the report does not otherwise\nhold: 1.5 when the affected entity is not the one submitting the report,\n1.13 when the entity belongs to a group, 2.8 when the incident originates\nfrom a third-party provider or another financial entity, 3.1 once the\ncompetent authority has assigned a reference, 3.8 when the relevance\nthreshold for clients and financial counterparts is met, 3.9 to 3.11 when\nany transaction was affected, and so on. Give each when its description\nsays it applies; the package cannot tell, and does not ask.\n\n## Updating a report\n\nEach report updates the one before it, so keep one data directory per\nincident and amend it:\n\n1. For the initial notification, set `stage` to `initial notification`\n   and fill in sections 1 and 2.\n2. For the intermediate report, set `stage` to `intermediate report`,\n   add section 3's fields, and correct anything in sections 1 and 2 that\n   has changed. 2.1, the incident's reference code, never changes.\n3. For the final report, set `stage` to `final report` and add section 4.\n\nA field due only at a later stage may be given early, as soon as it is\nknown. It is then checked against its type, and the fields it makes\nmandatory become due at the stage the glossary makes them due.\n\nDates and times are in UTC, written `2026-03-02T06:40:00Z`. Durations are\ndays, hours and minutes, written `DD:HH:MM`. Amounts are in the currency\n1.15 names, and percentages are numbers from 0 to 100.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HT63AztPXPn4kBCi8AHSvf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
